(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-asthma-controllers-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Asthma Controllers: Steroids, Leukotriene Modifiers, Mast Cell Stabilizers & Biologics Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which receptor does montelukast block?","choices":["High-affinity immunoglobulin E receptor","Muscarinic receptor","Adenosine A2B receptor","Cysteinyl leukotriene 1 receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast is a leukotriene D4 antagonist at the cysteinyl leukotriene 1 receptor, which mediates inflammation, bronchial constriction and mucus secretion. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Leukotrienes are made from arachidonic acid through which enzyme?","choices":["Cyclooxygenase","Phosphodiesterase","5-lipoxygenase","Myosin light chain kinase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Arachidonic acid is converted by 5-lipoxygenase to leukotriene A4 and on to leukotrienes B4, C4, D4 and E4; cyclooxygenase makes the prostaglandins and thromboxane. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A patient who has needed several courses of oral corticosteroids now has trouble climbing stairs because of leg weakness. Which listed side effect fits?","choices":["Adrenal suppression","Steroid myopathy","Peripheral neuritis","Immunosuppression"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Muscle weakness (steroid myopathy) is a listed systemic corticosteroid side effect, alongside adrenal suppression, growth suppression, dermal thinning, hypertension, hyperglycemia, mood change and immunosuppression. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which systemic corticosteroid side effect is the reason a longer course is tapered rather than stopped abruptly?","choices":["Growth suppression","Steroid myopathy","Hyperglycemia","Adrenal suppression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroids cause adrenal suppression, so the general rule is to taper a longer course rather than stop it suddenly. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Why are oral systemic corticosteroids reserved mainly for asthma exacerbations?","choices":["They are the least potent option","They take up to 8 weeks to work","Their side effects limit their use","They must be taken before exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral systemic corticosteroids are the most potent option for suppressing airway inflammation, but their side effects limit their use, so they are used in asthma exacerbations. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Fluticasone (Flovent) belongs to which drug class?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Long-acting beta-2 agonists","Leukotriene modifiers","Mast cell stabilizers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inhaled corticosteroids are beclomethasone, budesonide, flunisolide, fluticasone (Flovent), mometasone and triamcinolone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"When must cromolyn be taken to prevent an asthma attack?","choices":["At the start of an attack","After symptoms appear","Before antigen exposure","During an exacerbation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mast cell stabilizers such as cromolyn must be taken before the antigen insult; they remove the trigger rather than reverse bronchospasm. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Dupilumab (Dupixent) and mepolizumab (Nucala), the biologics for eosinophilic asthma, block which kind of target?","choices":["Immunoglobulin E","Interleukins","Leukotriene D4 receptors","Phosphodiesterase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are anti-interleukin biologics (anti-interleukin-4 and anti-interleukin-5) for the eosinophilic phenotype; omalizumab, by contrast, binds immunoglobulin E. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Among the oral systemic corticosteroids for asthma, which one is listed as an oral liquid rather than a tablet?","choices":["Prednisone","Methylprednisolone","Prednisolone","Fluticasone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prednisolone (Ora-Pred) is listed as the oral liquid; prednisone (Deltasone) is the oral tablet, and methylprednisolone and dexamethasone are oral or parenteral. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A patient starting a controller inhaler is told it can take up to 8 weeks to reach its maximal effect. Which drug group is this?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Mast cell stabilizers","Short-acting beta-2 agonists","Anticholinergics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids inhibit the inflammatory cascade and can take up to 8 weeks for maximal effects, which is why controllers are used daily rather than for quick relief. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of inhaled corticosteroids is an infection in the mouth?","choices":["Dry mouth","Heartburn","Injection site pain","Oral candidiasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral candidiasis (thrush) is an inhaled corticosteroid adverse effect, along with growth suppression in children at high dose and inhibition of the immune system. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Symbicort, Dulera and Advair each pair which two drug classes?","choices":["A short-acting beta-2 agonist and an anticholinergic","A long-acting beta-2 agonist and an inhaled corticosteroid","A long-acting beta-2 agonist and an anticholinergic","An inhaled corticosteroid and a leukotriene modifier"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each combines a long-acting beta-2 agonist (formoterol or salmeterol) with an inhaled corticosteroid (budesonide, mometasone or fluticasone). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How does omalizumab prevent mast cell degranulation?","choices":["It binds circulating immunoglobulin E","It stabilizes the mast cell membrane","It blocks interleukin-5","It blocks leukotriene receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Omalizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody, binds circulating immunoglobulin E, which decreases the high-affinity receptors on mast cells and prevents degranulation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which asthma biologic is given intravenously?","choices":["Reslizumab","Omalizumab","Mepolizumab","Dupilumab"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reslizumab (Cinqair), one of the anti-interleukin-5 biologics, is given intravenously. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which long-term risk is listed among omalizumab's adverse effects?","choices":["Growth suppression","Secondary malignancies","Adrenal suppression","Peripheral neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Omalizumab's listed adverse effects are gastrointestinal disturbances, anaphylaxis, neutralizing antibodies and secondary malignancies. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"By which route is montelukast taken?","choices":["By inhalation","By subcutaneous injection","By intravenous infusion","By mouth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast is an oral leukotriene antagonist, so unlike the inhaled controllers it is taken by mouth. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"}]};
